# Code owners for files whose changes can silently weaken a gate: the confidentiality
# gates, every validator and the check list CI runs, the hooks, and the review that
# judges a PR.
#
# Binding only when branch protection on `main` requires "Review from Code Owners"
# (a repository admin setting, not something this file can turn on).

# All of tools/validate/, so all of what it holds:
#   - every validator, the customer-reference and secrets gates among them, with
#     their allowlists, grandfathered exemptions and baselines;
#   - the check list CI runs (checks.py) and its runner (run_checks.py): dropping an
#     entry, or marking one soft, removes a CI gate without touching the workflow;
#   - pr-ready's logic and the evidence block it writes (pr_ready.py, evidence.py);
#   - the shared helpers the gates import, such as _git.py, which the customer-
#     reference gate reads staged content and history through;
#   - the tests and known-bad fixtures that pin what each gate must catch.
# The directory, not a list of files: a gate runs as `python3 tools/validate/<name>.py`,
# so Python puts this directory first on sys.path, and a NEW module added here (a
# `json.py`, a `yaml/` package) would replace what an owned gate imports without
# editing any owned file.
/tools/validate/                                             @djwaldo

# Hooks and their installer: a change here can disable every local gate.
/scripts/install-hooks.sh                                    @djwaldo
/scripts/pre-commit.sh                                       @djwaldo
/scripts/commit-msg.sh                                       @djwaldo

# The maintainer's pre-review customer-name check. Main's copy is the one run on
# every contributor PR, so a change to it changes every later PR's check.
/scripts/check-pr.sh                                         @djwaldo

# CI, where the gate is the backstop for --no-verify and uninstalled hooks.
/.github/workflows/validate.yml                              @djwaldo

# The contributor's pre-PR run of the check list: the wrapper, and the interpreter
# choice it shares with pre-commit. Its logic is pr_ready.py, owned above.
/scripts/pr-ready.sh                                         @djwaldo
/scripts/lib/python-bin.sh                                   @djwaldo

# The pr-ready skill and the reviewer it dispatches: the brief decides what a
# review looks for, so trimming it removes review areas without touching a gate.
/.claude/agents/pr-reviewer.md                               @djwaldo
/.claude/skills/pr-ready/                                    @djwaldo

# The SessionStart hook that warns when the git hooks are missing.
/.claude/settings.json                                       @djwaldo

# This file itself, so ownership cannot be removed without review.
/.github/CODEOWNERS                                          @djwaldo
